ly opened a gap, but was then chased down by Ethiopia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/67372"} "Derartu Tulu"] ". Together they had broken the field and ran together until the bell. Then Tulu pulled away and Meyer could not respond, Tulu winning by almost six seconds. American " [:a {:href "/athletes/77878"} "Lynn Jennings"] " ran a huge PR to get the bronze medal, while McColgan finished in fifth place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold and silver medal won by Tulu and Meyer had special sociologic implications. Tulu was a black African, while Meyer was a white South African. South African had been banned from the Olympics since 1964, because of the treatment of blacks in that nation, and Meyer’s silver medal was the first for her nation since 1960. They did not know each other well. But at the finish line, Tulu waited for Meyer to trail in behind her and they then ran together, hand-in-hand, on a victory lap. David Wallechinsky has written that this “seemed to symbolize hope for a new Africa”."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "472"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/67372"} "Derartu Tulu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ETH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ETH.png"}] "ETH"]] [:td {} "31:55.67 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "31:06.02 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1474"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/75234"} "Elana Meyer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/RSA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/RSA.png"}] "RSA"]] [:td {} "32:05.45 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "31:11.75 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silve